{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q021_nad_binding_convergent_evolution","answer":"NAD(P) binding is an evolutionarily reusable capability, not a marker of a single homologous lineage. Its occurrence in different SCOPe folds and superfamilies is consistent with repeated independent recruitment of the cofactor—convergent evolution. Within a homologous family or superfamily, diversification of substrate specificity and catalytic activity illustrates divergent evolution. Different fold assignments alone do not prove independent origins: very ancient homology and substantial structural divergence can be difficult to recognize.

SCOPe families group relatively close relatives; superfamilies infer deeper common ancestry. A shared fold describes structural topology without necessarily implying homology, and a shared class describes broad secondary-structure organization. For example, NAD(P)-binding Rossmann-fold domains (c.2) represent one structural solution, not all NAD(P)-binding enzymes.

The same cofactor imposes similar geometric and chemical constraints, so unrelated proteins can independently acquire similar binding loops, residue arrangements, or catalytic chemistry. Short motifs and local active-site resemblance therefore cannot establish homology. Stronger evidence comes from statistically significant sequence relationships across the domain, conserved detailed topology and unusual shared structural features, and coherent phylogenetic evidence. Cofactor or active-site similarity alone also cannot justify transferring substrate specificity, reaction assignment, or an entire structural template; those require independent functional or structural support.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
